    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.statusline\n\n### Description\n\nI actually don't know that this is a bug.  Maybe it's a feature.  If so can someone explain why this is desirable, as a relative newbie it's not clear to me.\r\n\r\nWhen I enter normal/insert/visual mode, the statusline notifies me of this immediately.  But if I enter visual-line or visual-block, the statusline stays on \"Normal\" until I key a movement, and then and only then does the mode on the statusline change to whichever of the two modes I selected.\r\n\r\nIf I start `nvim --clean`, `visual-line` and `visual-block` show immediately upon keying `<S-v>` & `<C-v>`, so this is a side-effect of this plugin, rather than something NeoVim does as standard.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.10.2\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -nu minimal.lua`\r\n2. <C-v>\r\n\r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nExpect status-line to show \"visual-block\"\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nStatus-line shows \"normal\"",
